#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128831 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "021e3349-c54d-55a8-9095-902bba933660" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Pythagorean Theorem to the Unit Circle"}] :question/id #uuid "323261a0-d9c6-587b-93bf-b2e831893af5" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-3479" :question/problem "The point $P$ is located in the second quadrant and lies on the unit circle.\nThe $y$-coordinate of $P$ is $\\dfrac{\\sqrt 2}{2}$. What is the $x$-coordinate of $P$?" :question/worked-solution "Since the point $P$ lies on the unit circle, it must satisfy the Pythagorean theorem:\n\n$$\nx^2+y^2=1\n$$\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{\\sqrt 2}{2}\n$$\n\ninto the above and solving for $x$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} + y^{2} &= 1 \\\\\nx^{2} + (\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})^{2} &= 1 \\\\\nx^{2} + \\frac{1}{2} &= 1 \\\\\nx^{2} &= 1 - \\frac{1}{2} \\\\\nx^{2} &= \\frac{1}{2} \\\\\nx &= ± \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\\nx &= ± \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we select the negative answer since $x$ is negative in the second quadrant. Therefore,\n\n$$\nx = -\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128832 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9089ba5c-f241-574e-9592-a26e09535b69" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Trigonometric Ratio Using the Pythagorean Identity"}] :question/id #uuid "a8e5384a-5baa-537f-9ec0-c775026f98d5" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-3481" :question/problem "The diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that $\\sin\\theta=-\\dfrac{3}{4}$, what is the value of $\\cos \\theta$?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2021/Source/Images/8b6363ef3d603f43d9169280502749cc.png)" :question/worked-solution "We can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\cos\\theta$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin^{2} θ + \\cos^{2} θ &= 1 \\\\\n\\cos^{2} θ &= 1 - \\sin^{2} θ \\\\\n\\cos θ &= ± \\sqrt{1 - \\sin^{2} θ}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\n\\sin\\theta = -\\dfrac{3}{4}\n$$\n\ngives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\cos θ &= ± \\sqrt{1 - (-\\frac{3}{4})^{2}} \\\\\n&= ± \\sqrt{1 - \\frac{9}{16}} \\\\\n&= ± \\sqrt{\\frac{7}{16}} \\\\\n&= ± \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the negative answer since $\\cos\\theta$ is negative in the third quadrant. So,\n\n$$\n\\cos\\theta = -\\dfrac {\\sqrt {7}}{4}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128833 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "98d6004a-547a-5b0b-9748-9571cc3ee632" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Tangent or Cotangent Using the Pythagorean Identity"}] :question/id #uuid "97f55dee-3f6b-5c1f-a901-b391ab37a0df" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-3483" :question/problem "The diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that $\\sin\\theta=-\\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{2}$, what is the value of $\\tan\\theta$?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2021/Source/Images/6e4741d820efa51e7f82e4abc1c518b4.png)" :question/worked-solution "We can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\cos\\theta$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin^{2} θ + \\cos^{2} θ &= 1 \\\\\n\\cos^{2} θ &= 1 - \\sin^{2} θ \\\\\n\\cos θ &= ± \\sqrt{1 - \\sin^{2} θ}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\n\\sin\\theta = -\\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{2}\n$$\n\ngives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\cos θ &= ± \\sqrt{1 - (-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})^{2}} \\\\\n&= ± \\sqrt{1 - \\frac{3}{4}} \\\\\n&= ± \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}} \\\\\n&= ± \\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $\\cos\\theta$ is positive in the fourth quadrant. So,\n\n$$\n\\cos\\theta = \\dfrac 1 2\n$$\n\nFinally, knowing that\n\n$$\n\\tan\\theta = \\dfrac{\\sin\\theta}{\\cos\\theta}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\tan\\theta = \\dfrac{\\left(-\\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{2}\\right)}{\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)} =-\\sqrt 3\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128834 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "55a09362-632a-56c4-b246-903e18fc77ee" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Reciprocal Trigonometric Ratio Using the Pythagorean Identity"}] :question/id #uuid "cfe7b419-9067-5b8c-b9c2-d4ec91120333" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-3486" :question/problem "The diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $-\\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{2}$, what is $\\csc\\theta$?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2021/Source/Images/7203bdaa2af0eb3b29f22c170b3c88e5.png)" :question/worked-solution "We're given that\n\n$$\nx=-\\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{2}\n$$\n\nat the point $P$. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\cos\\theta = -\\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{2}\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\sin\\theta$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin^{2} θ + \\cos^{2} θ &= 1 \\\\\n\\sin^{2} θ &= 1 - \\cos^{2} θ \\\\\n\\sin θ &= ± \\sqrt{1 - \\cos^{2} θ}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\n\\cos\\theta = -\\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{2}\n$$\n\ngives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin θ &= ± \\sqrt{1 - (-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})^{2}} \\\\\n&= ± \\sqrt{1 - \\frac{3}{4}} \\\\\n&= ± \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}} \\\\\n&= ± \\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the negative answer since $\\sin\\theta$ is negative in the third quadrant. So,\n\n$$\n\\sin\\theta = -\\dfrac 1 2\n$$\n\nFinally, knowing that\n\n$$\n\\csc\\theta = \\dfrac{1}{\\sin\\theta}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\csc\\theta = \\dfrac{1}{\\left(-\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)} =-2\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186245565 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "021e3349-c54d-55a8-9095-902bba933660" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Pythagorean Theorem to the Unit Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c79f603c-0992-57c1-a92b-967b97f1dd6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245567} {:answer/id #uuid "46291568-e7b4-552f-a6a4-3363c0c53cf4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245568} {:answer/id #uuid "753a7fdc-1606-5911-a1d6-cd3a6c42d063" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245569} {:answer/id #uuid "59d27608-00d3-5d2d-b10b-6304be2a914a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245570} {:answer/id #uuid "18367eaf-9c3c-5c23-b212-b5a1a11aa2cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245571}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "18367eaf-9c3c-5c23-b212-b5a1a11aa2cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245571} :answer-field/id #uuid "92afb87f-68d2-51fa-a9fb-1e6d6ba47676" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245566}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "249b4f98-ecec-5db6-9d63-40905e020bb5" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-124816" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13932638/assets/3687e362fea73defca43332ee70f232282ad1eded9dc14cbbc045de4324067f0.png)\n\nThe point $P$ is located on the unit circle, as shown above. The $y$-coordinate of $P$ is $-\\frac{6}{7}.$ What is the $x$-coordinate of $P?$" :question/worked-solution "Since the point $P$ lies on the unit circle, it must satisfy the Pythagorean theorem: \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y=-\\frac{6}{7}$ into the above and solving for $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{(-\\frac{6}{7})}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+(\\frac{36}{49}) & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =1-\\frac{36}{49} \\\\ {x}^{2} & =\\frac{13}{49} \\\\ x & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{13}{49}} \\\\ x & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we select the positive answer since $x$ is positive in the fourth quadrant. So our final answer is $x=\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{7}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186245572 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "021e3349-c54d-55a8-9095-902bba933660" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Pythagorean Theorem to the Unit Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b9303be6-fbde-55e7-aac3-b0fbb467966b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245574} {:answer/id #uuid "e41ad4ec-c579-57b1-8e76-eac339371a41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{4}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245575} {:answer/id #uuid "ab6d02a8-430a-5997-a791-d23c3e51a078" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245576} {:answer/id #uuid "0a255431-b930-594f-8ea5-8e204d2612a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245577} {:answer/id #uuid "f519bb5a-29fc-5ad1-b206-c7abc46852d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{2}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245578}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ab6d02a8-430a-5997-a791-d23c3e51a078" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245576} :answer-field/id #uuid "09440c6d-ab95-55b1-a2c8-609be0538b67" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245573}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4d634ece-12cb-5dee-afd7-34c2cffd0816" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-124822" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13932638/assets/7d466b53d645568256cc6d16b5b28048bdadc6c8977be15631f5e26cbe4ec6ba.png)\n\nThe point $P$ is located on the unit circle, as shown above. The $y$-coordinate of $P$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}.$ What is the $x$-coordinate of $P?$" :question/worked-solution "Since the point $P$ lies on the unit circle, it must satisfy the Pythagorean theorem: \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}$ into the above and solving for $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3})}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+(\\frac{5}{9}) & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =1-\\frac{5}{9} \\\\ {x}^{2} & =\\frac{4}{9} \\\\ x & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{4}{9}} \\\\ x & =\\pm \\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we select the negative answer since $x$ is negative in the second quadrant. So our final answer is $x=-\\frac{2}{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186245579 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "021e3349-c54d-55a8-9095-902bba933660" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Pythagorean Theorem to the Unit Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "abb90a8f-5707-5f39-8cc8-6d845353c2a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{2\\sqrt{14}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245581} {:answer/id #uuid "fef2536b-33d4-50f9-ae6c-2f7008a2b359" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{2\\sqrt{14}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245582} {:answer/id #uuid "a68c87e6-59a2-5ba6-aedf-f80459fc1cd0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{2}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245583} {:answer/id #uuid "097d4ba8-ff85-5627-95dc-605d48d95a5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245584} {:answer/id #uuid "d4e5fec8-1fa7-5008-a74b-64b68cdce11b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{2}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245585}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fef2536b-33d4-50f9-ae6c-2f7008a2b359" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{2\\sqrt{14}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245582} :answer-field/id #uuid "f3abf1af-1c66-5ee3-858d-46b6df2ff6db" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245580}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4bb78e36-0530-5dbb-9306-ce623b4b8557" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-124828" :question/problem "The point $P$ is located in the fourth quadrant and lies on the unit circle. The $y$-coordinate of $P$ is $-\\frac{5}{9}.$ What is the $x$-coordinate of $P?$" :question/worked-solution "Since the point $P$ lies on the unit circle, it must satisfy the Pythagorean theorem: \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y=-\\frac{5}{9}$ into the above and solving for $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{(-\\frac{5}{9})}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+(\\frac{25}{81}) & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =1-\\frac{25}{81} \\\\ {x}^{2} & =\\frac{56}{81} \\\\ x & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{56}{81}} \\\\ x & =\\pm \\frac{2\\sqrt{14}}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we select the positive answer since $x$ is positive in the fourth quadrant. So our final answer is $x=\\frac{2\\sqrt{14}}{9}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186245586 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9089ba5c-f241-574e-9592-a26e09535b69" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Trigonometric Ratio Using the Pythagorean Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07121185-336b-5744-b3eb-7033a4c7e7e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245588} {:answer/id #uuid "b3f8cfff-141e-5381-90ac-03b6f38f2c13" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245589} {:answer/id #uuid "b046a43b-d398-5a2b-abd7-befe5112dfcb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245590} {:answer/id #uuid "2ded9f8f-cd3a-5f87-936d-9610a17302b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "cos⁡\\theta =-\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245591} {:answer/id #uuid "1f8c596f-1e74-5c15-950a-5fa06525d1d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "cos⁡\\theta =-\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245592}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "07121185-336b-5744-b3eb-7033a4c7e7e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245588} :answer-field/id #uuid "fda69e35-0662-5ecb-93a0-6aed06b94d50" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245587}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "998f15db-1b74-586a-8dd5-838041bed908" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-124957" :question/problem "If $sin⁡\\theta =-\\frac{1}{5}$ and ${270}^{∘}<\\theta <{360}^{∘}$ then what is the value of $cos⁡\\theta ?$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13932638/assets/01f881c353d1d113e97680cce375359cedf561e69259a6b2ba1fe15097742dda.png)\n\nWe're given that $sin⁡\\theta =-\\frac{1}{5}.$ We can use the Pythagorean identity to find $cos⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $sin⁡\\theta =-\\frac{1}{5}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{1}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{24}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $cos⁡\\theta $ is positive in the fourth quadrant. So, $cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{5}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186245593 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9089ba5c-f241-574e-9592-a26e09535b69" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Trigonometric Ratio Using the Pythagorean Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b99f5d0c-8ebe-5040-84ef-9e3b963d49a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "sin⁡\\theta =-\\frac{3\\sqrt{6}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245595} {:answer/id #uuid "4d336c51-9a12-5c64-88a1-d59e87b8f7ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245596} {:answer/id #uuid "36ff4a5d-a510-5394-b0de-3d72d5e17a75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "sin⁡\\theta =-\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245597} {:answer/id #uuid "d8f94acf-54c7-5975-8fda-a733ae4d2ce0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{3\\sqrt{6}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245598} {:answer/id #uuid "d550743b-2420-5e5f-a1e8-404fb4dc41de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245599}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4d336c51-9a12-5c64-88a1-d59e87b8f7ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245596} :answer-field/id #uuid "232836a6-dec0-5dd4-97be-cff888d45229" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245594}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "32b575e9-a555-53bc-a62c-e87c7ca0a623" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-124958" :question/problem "If $cos⁡\\theta =-\\frac{1}{4}$ and $\\frac{\\pi }{2}<\\theta <\\pi $ then what is the value of $sin⁡\\theta ?$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13932638/assets/7e887638bb8b3b135c6a8e6eecf564fe8310a43f2ce26f8b565ec6966bafb89c.png)\n\nWe're given that $cos⁡\\theta =-\\frac{1}{4}.$ We can use the Pythagorean identity to find $sin⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $cos⁡\\theta =-\\frac{1}{4}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{1}{16}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{15}{16}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $sin⁡\\theta $ is positive in the second quadrant. So, $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{4}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186245600 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9089ba5c-f241-574e-9592-a26e09535b69" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Trigonometric Ratio Using the Pythagorean Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cbf61191-2fc0-576a-acad-8549a53c5231" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "cos⁡\\theta =-\\frac{5\\sqrt{3}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245602} {:answer/id #uuid "029d2f02-5172-5d88-9634-f4476bd202ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245603} {:answer/id #uuid "08b0afcf-b13c-5ad6-a58f-3077a4d81714" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245604} {:answer/id #uuid "abe56fe0-1ece-5bcf-8620-c851a9d6641d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "cos⁡\\theta =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245605} {:answer/id #uuid "d70cbc17-3d92-507c-ad23-665bb44a38e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "cos⁡\\theta =-\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245606}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d70cbc17-3d92-507c-ad23-665bb44a38e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "cos⁡\\theta =-\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245606} :answer-field/id #uuid "b322c07d-a35c-559f-bd95-5eb18f792cbd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245601}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "17f4ca73-6ab9-502a-a923-dbf0c7fc4e20" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-124959" :question/problem "If $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{5}{6}$ and ${90}^{∘}<\\theta <{180}^{∘}$ then what is the value of $cos⁡\\theta ?$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13932638/assets/f6c2b0f2e6b8c9b10de0341a73d8c7d1d88ba64bd046e82fee8db37828ca2555.png)\n\nWe're given that $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{5}{6}.$ We can use the Pythagorean identity to find $cos⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{5}{6}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{5}{6})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{25}{36}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{11}{36}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the negative answer since $cos⁡\\theta $ is negative in the second quadrant. So, $cos⁡\\theta =-\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{6}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186245607 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "98d6004a-547a-5b0b-9748-9571cc3ee632" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Tangent or Cotangent Using the Pythagorean Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ccc3428-55ff-5a1f-81dd-aaa2cfe42bb3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "tan⁡\\theta =\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245609} {:answer/id #uuid "b2dfc134-7e11-550c-a7c8-f48893b1651e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "tan⁡\\theta =-2\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245610} {:answer/id #uuid "880698f7-3783-521d-b8de-ae4d00eb8eda" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "tan⁡\\theta =-\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245611} {:answer/id #uuid "cd5f75e8-ecb3-5836-a29d-508998a836bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "tan⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245612} {:answer/id #uuid "6429f111-191f-5580-bd60-336e44760393" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "tan⁡\\theta =2\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245613}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6429f111-191f-5580-bd60-336e44760393" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "tan⁡\\theta =2\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245613} :answer-field/id #uuid "94778196-b324-5dea-a721-bf43032dec60" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245608}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "384f6e11-34d2-50a8-bed1-2ca99e5088c6" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-124974" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13932638/assets/bab7b997049effa2d60ffdd37bbc4f79cf251add61b9ce9b8877c88054fa6d3d.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that $cos⁡\\theta =-\\frac{1}{5},$ what is the value of $tan⁡\\theta ?$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13932638/assets/85975fdcee2c92cd4ca6ba0074738982b6cef01c8eaab14cb44fabd6c0615ba2.png)\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $sin⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $cos⁡\\theta =-\\frac{1}{5}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{1}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{24}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the negative answer since $sin⁡\\theta $ is negative in the third quadrant. So, $sin⁡\\theta =-\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{5}.$\n\nFinally, knowing that $tan⁡\\theta =\\frac{sin⁡\\theta }{cos⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\ntan⁡\\theta =\\frac{(-\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{5})}{(-\\frac{1}{5})}=2\\sqrt{6}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186245614 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "98d6004a-547a-5b0b-9748-9571cc3ee632" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Tangent or Cotangent Using the Pythagorean Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "378a1786-d6fe-5416-96e0-72d552588e8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "tan⁡\\theta =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245616} {:answer/id #uuid "ab341c54-2d4d-5522-9b8a-25e79ce2aa73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "tan⁡\\theta =-\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245617} {:answer/id #uuid "4a062462-5ef8-50a8-b795-a0385f9fc9e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "tan⁡\\theta =\\frac{15}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245618} {:answer/id #uuid "0418c16f-f938-5c8a-baab-2a4820f42606" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "tan⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245619} {:answer/id #uuid "ce116553-920f-5788-b35f-b829e481c989" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "tan⁡\\theta =\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245620}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ce116553-920f-5788-b35f-b829e481c989" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "tan⁡\\theta =\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245620} :answer-field/id #uuid "9536467a-e9df-56aa-b350-55b79a39743f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245615}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2d9ef966-b8f1-5433-8fc3-084844d45df8" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-124976" :question/problem "If $sin⁡\\theta =-\\frac{3}{5}$ and $\\pi <\\theta <\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$ then what is the value of $tan⁡\\theta ?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch our angle $\\theta $ in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13932638/assets/8761c66ecaef04f605c3c05b2274673b7b8de78763c224d795fbb000a5acc4b0.png)\n\nWe're given that $sin⁡\\theta =-\\frac{3}{5}.$ We can use the Pythagorean identity to find $cos⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $sin⁡\\theta =-\\frac{3}{5}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(-\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{9}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{16}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the negative answer since $cos⁡\\theta $ is negative in the third quadrant. So, $cos⁡\\theta =-\\frac{4}{5}.$\n\nFinally, knowing that $tan⁡\\theta =\\frac{sin⁡\\theta }{cos⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\ntan⁡\\theta =\\frac{(-\\frac{3}{5})}{(-\\frac{4}{5})}=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186245621 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "55a09362-632a-56c4-b246-903e18fc77ee" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Reciprocal Trigonometric Ratio Using the Pythagorean Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c7c24e3d-6803-5199-97e1-bd13410dc9e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "sec⁡\\theta =8\\sqrt{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245623} {:answer/id #uuid "d91ba099-7743-5942-9cf0-694b5a2d9cec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "sec⁡\\theta =\\frac{8\\sqrt{15}}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245624} {:answer/id #uuid "706dc955-dc51-5468-b03b-22112ebb4568" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "sec⁡\\theta =-\\frac{8\\sqrt{15}}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245625} {:answer/id #uuid "de5028d1-048e-518e-b4dd-482c0872a56b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "sec⁡\\theta =-\\frac{4\\sqrt{15}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245626} {:answer/id #uuid "eba51077-bb3f-52cf-ade7-6ecc08816110" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "sec⁡\\theta =-8\\sqrt{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245627}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "706dc955-dc51-5468-b03b-22112ebb4568" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "sec⁡\\theta =-\\frac{8\\sqrt{15}}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245625} :answer-field/id #uuid "0c5cf0b1-5600-5e1e-a6d7-f7cac1c521dd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245622}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c006e542-29a2-5102-8308-98f4920ebd17" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-125004" :question/problem "If $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{7}{8}$ and $\\frac{\\pi }{2}<\\theta <\\pi $ then what is the value of $sec⁡\\theta ?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch our angle $\\theta $ in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13932638/assets/5c98414bbe0e1e373864e9ec1d6b8667bb0032c8824f20bed1e6f9b9079c4d02.png)\n\nWe're given that $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{7}{8}.$ We can use the Pythagorean identity to find $cos⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{7}{8}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{7}{8})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{49}{64}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{15}{64}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the negative answer since $cos⁡\\theta $ is negative in the second quadrant. So, $cos⁡\\theta =-\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{8}.$\n\nFinally, knowing that $sec⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{cos⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\nsec⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{(-\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{8})}=-\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{15}}=-\\frac{8\\sqrt{15}}{15}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129502 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "021e3349-c54d-55a8-9095-902bba933660" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Pythagorean Theorem to the Unit Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "99770af6-3f14-5f15-9c64-adaa0914065e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = \\frac{\\sqrt{31}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126804} {:answer/id #uuid "cb71c16a-93cd-51f7-8735-bbcce913df55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y =-\\frac{\\sqrt{21}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126805} {:answer/id #uuid "210bab46-eae2-585f-9604-bd0292627685" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = \\frac{\\sqrt{21}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126806} {:answer/id #uuid "27641fda-91a5-531c-ad49-533d6b6af2b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y =-\\frac{\\sqrt{531}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126807} {:answer/id #uuid "5753c5ac-3eb8-572f-a23c-23fc8822b353" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y =-\\frac{\\sqrt{31}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126808}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cb71c16a-93cd-51f7-8735-bbcce913df55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y =-\\frac{\\sqrt{21}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126805} :answer-field/id #uuid "4416919b-59ab-5978-9c10-142b91797ae6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126803}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e4be94e7-eb10-5b1f-8a9d-fe5e79ca236b" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-23273" :question/problem "The point $P$ is located in the third quadrant and lies on the unit circle. The $x$-coordinate of $P$ is $-\\frac{2}{5}$. What is the $y$-coordinate of $P$?" :question/worked-solution "Since the point $P$ lies on the unit circle, it must satisfy the Pythagorean theorem: \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-\\frac{2}{5}$ into the above and solving for $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {(-\\frac{2}{5})}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ (\\frac{4}{25})+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =1-\\frac{4}{25} \\\\ {y}^{2} & =\\frac{21}{25} \\\\ y & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{21}{25}} \\\\ y & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{21}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we select the negative answer since $y$ is negative in the third quadrant. So our final answer is $y=-\\frac{\\sqrt{21}}{5}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186245628 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "021e3349-c54d-55a8-9095-902bba933660" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Pythagorean Theorem to the Unit Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a56d1d3-abf2-5c7b-8133-ed6a5a0360e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\sqrt{29}}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245630} {:answer/id #uuid "7da59c67-2616-5302-95c4-796b28547383" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{\\sqrt{23}}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245631} {:answer/id #uuid "81ab70de-abf8-5e3d-889e-06ee1bea01c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\sqrt{23}}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245632} {:answer/id #uuid "a329c9c8-ff11-5a9f-9193-8ffaa4041b64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{7\\sqrt{33}}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245633} {:answer/id #uuid "3b0673b7-0798-5c63-8942-39a2f614e2e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{\\sqrt{29}}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245634}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7da59c67-2616-5302-95c4-796b28547383" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{\\sqrt{23}}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245631} :answer-field/id #uuid "b54e2fb8-9b8d-5227-b132-f4bd55a8d3c7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245629}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "367d3472-d63c-5a7e-b707-c09fd7abc7ae" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-23289" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13932638/assets/8904a5ebafade54a46cee77a2e20cac255c888d257ef1c208b2def32d247f2cd.png)\n\nThe point $P$ is located on the unit circle, as shown above. The $y$-coordinate of $P$ is $\\frac{11}{12}.$ What is the $x$-coordinate of $P?$" :question/worked-solution "Since the point $P$ lies on the unit circle, it must satisfy the Pythagorean theorem: \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y=\\frac{11}{12}$ into the above and solving for $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{(\\frac{11}{12})}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+(\\frac{121}{144}) & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =1-\\frac{121}{144} \\\\ {x}^{2} & =\\frac{23}{144} \\\\ x & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{23}{144}} \\\\ x & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{23}}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we select the negative answer since $x$ is negative in the second quadrant. So our final answer is $x=-\\frac{\\sqrt{23}}{12}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186245635 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "98d6004a-547a-5b0b-9748-9571cc3ee632" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Tangent or Cotangent Using the Pythagorean Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "720fdb6d-0ffe-58c0-bf61-1370476fc1a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "tan⁡\\theta =2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245637} {:answer/id #uuid "01d54a3f-fa32-5083-8bce-342eb2458b06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "tan⁡\\theta =7\\sqrt{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245638} {:answer/id #uuid "3e51042d-0f02-5fce-8719-5caa1143e93c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "tan⁡\\theta =-\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245639} {:answer/id #uuid "33b4bcea-a773-572c-a039-6601a000aaa4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "tan⁡\\theta =\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245640} {:answer/id #uuid "ca1bb7a0-028e-5816-aa81-8c8c1b748b29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "tan⁡\\theta =-2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245641}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "33b4bcea-a773-572c-a039-6601a000aaa4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "tan⁡\\theta =\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245640} :answer-field/id #uuid "9ab19f61-8d62-54ca-8240-55f433bbf740" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245636}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "bbfc2efa-5938-583d-816d-de413216e824" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-23307" :question/problem "If $cos⁡\\theta =-\\frac{1}{2}$ and ${180}^{∘}<\\theta <{270}^{∘},$ then what is the value of $tan⁡\\theta ?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch our angle $\\theta $ in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13932638/assets/7873579bb5dd1976330ec24f13b30dd384f916cf23bbf48aac4b2a522ee9548a.png)\n\nWe're given that $cos⁡\\theta =-\\frac{1}{2}.$ We can use the Pythagorean identity to find $sin⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $cos⁡\\theta =-\\frac{1}{2}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{1}{4}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{3}{4}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the negative answer since $sin⁡\\theta $ is negative in the third quadrant. So, $sin⁡\\theta =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.$\n\nFinally, knowing that $tan⁡\\theta =\\frac{sin⁡\\theta }{cos⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\ntan⁡\\theta =\\frac{(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})}{(-\\frac{1}{2})}=\\sqrt{3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186245642 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9089ba5c-f241-574e-9592-a26e09535b69" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Trigonometric Ratio Using the Pythagorean Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e649a718-8089-5057-86ce-5c18e98cf28d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "cos⁡\\theta =-\\frac{12}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245644} {:answer/id #uuid "cc8f428e-9527-5ba5-8ada-550f155b4021" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{15}{17}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245645} {:answer/id #uuid "9f9d2fc5-af54-5124-b179-3c17131fae41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{7}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245646} {:answer/id #uuid "a1103f72-a2fc-5715-b6a3-767dd92b21f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{12}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245647} {:answer/id #uuid "dcb14ef5-7b71-51a8-af84-c7a527604517" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "cos⁡\\theta =-\\frac{15}{17}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245648}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e649a718-8089-5057-86ce-5c18e98cf28d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "cos⁡\\theta =-\\frac{12}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245644} :answer-field/id #uuid "09387f15-4d0c-5e19-a1f8-3336c40e9920" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245643}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bf22114b-65ea-5d8a-859d-2c70937236ca" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-24074" :question/problem "If $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{5}{13}$ and $\\frac{\\pi }{2}<\\theta <\\pi $ then what is the value of $cos⁡\\theta ?$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13932638/assets/d6ec4c0b71e64cd659bceea63a49f0ef09c44ee1c31353fd377963d7cdf3c3db.png)\n\nWe're given that $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{5}{13}.$ We can use the Pythagorean identity to find $cos⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{5}{13}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{5}{13})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{25}{169}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{144}{169}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{12}{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the negative answer since $cos⁡\\theta $ is negative in the second quadrant. So, $cos⁡\\theta =-\\frac{12}{13}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129507 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9089ba5c-f241-574e-9592-a26e09535b69" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Trigonometric Ratio Using the Pythagorean Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b7d2afde-e3f0-5dbf-aea4-93defa39143d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\cos θ = \\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126829} {:answer/id #uuid "f36ff079-69b4-5b86-b7b7-b5e0d1a54c57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\cos θ = \\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126830} {:answer/id #uuid "00df5428-714a-5e17-8c44-4e549984feb5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\cos θ =-\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126831} {:answer/id #uuid "240a3697-1465-56db-aaed-be4fa37670a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\cos θ =-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126832} {:answer/id #uuid "46f4421e-3c52-561b-964e-4a5681726de9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\cos θ =-\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126833}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "00df5428-714a-5e17-8c44-4e549984feb5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\cos θ =-\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126831} :answer-field/id #uuid "ece78389-9690-5bb8-94a4-3417d852ae22" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126828}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "83599989-7e1a-5f2f-adf9-0de9e207ef83" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-24247" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2021/Source/Images/q-24247.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that $\\sin θ = \\frac{1}{3}$, what is the value of $\\cos θ$?" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13932638/assets/4942cec8983dd2bddddd0ba2f8f9332b1b82f3999a44adb138b905457d39c633.png)\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $cos⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{3}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{1}{9}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{8}{9}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the negative answer since $cos⁡\\theta $ is negative in the second quadrant. So, $cos⁡\\theta =-\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129712 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "98d6004a-547a-5b0b-9748-9571cc3ee632" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Tangent or Cotangent Using the Pythagorean Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c61dc7c0-d3f8-5a52-a767-d5a5950ed61b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\tan θ =-4\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128067} {:answer/id #uuid "3c3acfbd-450b-529e-9e81-f6898d0afc15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\tan θ =-\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128068} {:answer/id #uuid "425120b8-9a7a-509b-b2b1-16f549aee0d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\tan θ = \\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128069} {:answer/id #uuid "51495d9a-03f0-5f49-aeab-3c6ea5811fbe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\tan θ =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128070} {:answer/id #uuid "681b517b-c0da-555d-9464-011b10f62e6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\tan θ = \\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128071}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "51495d9a-03f0-5f49-aeab-3c6ea5811fbe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\tan θ =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128070} :answer-field/id #uuid "a1f2f1dc-9d19-53e5-af38-cc9ea5f6ad94" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128066}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "299c91a4-6289-5d24-ace1-495b52634a62" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-42043" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2021/Source/Images/q-42043.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that $\\sin θ = \\frac{1}{2}$, what is the value of $\\tan θ$?" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13932638/assets/948610f67267e6ec1b845d2b4e1bc4030b81a1080bcb9a44d9f974ff78c3f7b0.png)\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $cos⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{2}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{1}{4}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{3}{4}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the negative answer since $cos⁡\\theta $ is negative in the second quadrant. So, $cos⁡\\theta =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.$\n\nFinally, knowing that $tan⁡\\theta =\\frac{sin⁡\\theta }{cos⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\ntan⁡\\theta =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{2})}{(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})}=-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186245649 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "98d6004a-547a-5b0b-9748-9571cc3ee632" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Tangent or Cotangent Using the Pythagorean Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bf3a3d97-4837-50da-a9e1-b7cfd036e0f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "cot⁡\\theta =\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245651} {:answer/id #uuid "cef2af1d-46e0-566b-9167-747540ec3b2d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "cot⁡\\theta =-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245652} {:answer/id #uuid "dd133afa-bffd-5b85-a17c-fc17cd6cf217" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "cot⁡\\theta =-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245653} {:answer/id #uuid "f7f11821-543c-5e81-8ab1-d0e0dfc23019" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "cot⁡\\theta =-\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245654} {:answer/id #uuid "d4332546-f32b-5ae6-a573-a1b36052cdce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "cot⁡\\theta =\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245655}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f7f11821-543c-5e81-8ab1-d0e0dfc23019" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "cot⁡\\theta =-\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245654} :answer-field/id #uuid "f5e7f63a-ac71-5409-93fb-da87576344d7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245650}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "37e87bc7-80c8-5654-8c99-e96b9d9f6c6d" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-85640" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13932638/assets/97c98411b3455c78c9e061cf54fa76eabfccc369e9e364a5366eb58bd5d2935d.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that $cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{2}{7},$ what is the value of $cot⁡\\theta ?$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13932638/assets/8c7889d63cf5894236bc5f5e24feb64ecf5a47acb2c07c807a4e65458a991232.png)\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $sin⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{2}{7}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{2}{7})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{4}{49}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{45}{49}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the negative answer since $sin⁡\\theta $ is negative in the fourth quadrant. So, $sin⁡\\theta =-\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{7}.$\n\nFinally, knowing that $cot⁡\\theta =\\frac{cos⁡\\theta }{sin⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\ncot⁡\\theta =\\frac{(\\frac{2}{7})}{(-\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{7})}=-\\frac{2}{3\\sqrt{5}}=-\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{15}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186245656 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "55a09362-632a-56c4-b246-903e18fc77ee" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Reciprocal Trigonometric Ratio Using the Pythagorean Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44c9bab2-94b2-52c1-bc5e-6235db012a7f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "sec⁡\\theta =\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245658} {:answer/id #uuid "492eab21-3fb0-54c0-92c6-a731710f234b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "sec⁡\\theta =\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245659} {:answer/id #uuid "48a01b4d-e2c7-5f6c-b388-0e1a527bebb3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "sec⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245660} {:answer/id #uuid "d4c7f755-77f7-57b2-b14c-c305aa4561e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "sec⁡\\theta =-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245661} {:answer/id #uuid "a4f81c0b-2967-5a6e-a74a-2763b2e55dfb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "sec⁡\\theta =-\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245662}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "492eab21-3fb0-54c0-92c6-a731710f234b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "sec⁡\\theta =\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245659} :answer-field/id #uuid "bee8e07c-bea9-57ef-8ea7-55ee192590f6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245657}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7e671aac-3a60-5948-a969-9b2f942ef09c" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-85641" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13932638/assets/9d879cc44b4c3949f38584482261cba8c97a58457ffeb611bfd313b7b3194b0a.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $y$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3},$ what is the value of $sec⁡\\theta ?$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13932638/assets/b79868db47bbe4ef308975cea659dc7b964ae7fcf894ee92bbb9ee8070dcaac1.png)\n\nWe're given that $y=-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, $sin⁡\\theta =-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}.$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $cos⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $sin⁡\\theta =-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{5}{9}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{4}{9}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $cos⁡\\theta $ is positive in the fourth quadrant. So, $cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{2}{3}.$\n\nFinally, knowing that $sec⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{cos⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\nsec⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{2}{3})}=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186245663 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "55a09362-632a-56c4-b246-903e18fc77ee" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Reciprocal Trigonometric Ratio Using the Pythagorean Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "60fe65a8-b139-5de1-92a2-f1c282bb17ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "csc⁡\\theta =\\frac{4}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245665} {:answer/id #uuid "f362d121-8787-5369-933a-4c74b57361ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "csc⁡\\theta =-\\frac{3}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245666} {:answer/id #uuid "49e5c347-96bc-53cf-8dd8-acdaf1551de4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "csc⁡\\theta =\\frac{4\\sqrt{3}}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245667} {:answer/id #uuid "8295e07f-60f7-5394-8cd2-aa6f2330f534" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "csc⁡\\theta =-\\frac{3\\sqrt{15}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245668} {:answer/id #uuid "c612bfa0-bace-5469-8960-152a2e1372aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "csc⁡\\theta =-\\frac{4\\sqrt{15}}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245669}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c612bfa0-bace-5469-8960-152a2e1372aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "csc⁡\\theta =-\\frac{4\\sqrt{15}}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245669} :answer-field/id #uuid "4aba410c-61c1-5e3d-b11a-7b607a678449" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245664}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bd526035-dea8-5fa0-8a08-1ec8fea39cc7" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-85642" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13932638/assets/75b3a8b6aa11ac54edd5e3f0d694514cf7e63eefd05313ab854ddd917aa32ad5.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{1}{4},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $csc⁡\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13932638/assets/73972bbedd114db6635eaec98dd0989f7d6e877aa276b381973b753204e2b8e8.png)\n\nWe're given that $x=\\frac{1}{4}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, $cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{4}.$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $sin⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{4}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{1}{16}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{15}{16}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we select the negative answer since $sin⁡\\theta $ is negative in the fourth quadrant. So $sin⁡\\theta =-\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{4}.$\n\nFinally, knowing that $csc⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{sin⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\ncsc⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{(-\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{4})}=-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{15}}=-\\frac{4\\sqrt{15}}{15}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186245670 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "55a09362-632a-56c4-b246-903e18fc77ee" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Reciprocal Trigonometric Ratio Using the Pythagorean Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f5f83147-6fbc-5ed2-bde7-45d9b85616dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "sec⁡\\theta =-\\frac{7\\sqrt{6}}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245672} {:answer/id #uuid "185b02c7-c0dc-5868-b578-ece8b1347e6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "sec⁡\\theta =-\\frac{7\\sqrt{5}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245673} {:answer/id #uuid "28690b26-9b29-56dd-8683-e436142a1c5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "sec⁡\\theta =-\\frac{7\\sqrt{3}}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245674} {:answer/id #uuid "01e39e5c-1b12-57ef-a0b8-efbe7b69e901" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "sec⁡\\theta =\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245675} {:answer/id #uuid "1e33194e-4c90-5549-be8f-b39afd15162d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "sec⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245676}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f5f83147-6fbc-5ed2-bde7-45d9b85616dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "sec⁡\\theta =-\\frac{7\\sqrt{6}}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245672} :answer-field/id #uuid "db0fcba2-580a-5416-a4f3-af3297439442" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245671}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0902cd66-9d18-50eb-a894-3b1edfc4b17b" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-85643" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13932638/assets/3be6fa94deb7fc38d870e5a31cc81c3cfd6b9ab84c21340e7e88f7246b1058bc.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $y$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{5}{7},$ what is the value of $sec⁡\\theta ?$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13932638/assets/17e6320115f2cf8b5ffad6abc099169774536af20d2559b453aaa97c1286d96d.png)\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{5}{7}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{5}{7}.$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $cos⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{5}{7}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{5}{7})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{25}{49}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{24}{49}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{24}}{7} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the negative answer since $cos⁡\\theta $ is negative in the second quadrant. So, $cos⁡\\theta =-\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{7}.$\n\nFinally, knowing that $sec⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{cos⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\nsec⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{(-\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{7})}=-\\frac{7}{2\\sqrt{6}}=-\\frac{7\\sqrt{6}}{12}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186245677 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "55a09362-632a-56c4-b246-903e18fc77ee" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Reciprocal Trigonometric Ratio Using the Pythagorean Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ac158b4-7c0a-58e8-949d-7db5d27652d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "sec⁡\\theta =-\\frac{3\\sqrt{7}}{18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245679} {:answer/id #uuid "46a30282-7d3f-56ba-9a99-e669ed7a5e4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "sec⁡\\theta =\\frac{5\\sqrt{3}}{21}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245680} {:answer/id #uuid "57b9d878-5132-5c86-925b-32a92dfdd9ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "sec⁡\\theta =-\\frac{4\\sqrt{5}}{21}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245681} {:answer/id #uuid "8d71834d-59e3-57b4-86ec-3e6080269d03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "sec⁡\\theta =\\frac{8\\sqrt{2}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245682} {:answer/id #uuid "40b81b0f-e8ee-5524-be62-4247258088fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "sec⁡\\theta =-\\frac{8\\sqrt{7}}{21}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245683}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "40b81b0f-e8ee-5524-be62-4247258088fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "sec⁡\\theta =-\\frac{8\\sqrt{7}}{21}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245683} :answer-field/id #uuid "c44c19a6-8c24-5e98-a45e-74d07f9eaefc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245678}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a15b0347-c7e8-56db-a50a-1d56900c2170" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-85649" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13932638/assets/cd41c969aee2e7c04c9034106978d8522d6a715733098bfde87abbdb861da7a8.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $y$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{1}{8},$ what is the value of $sec⁡\\theta ?$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13932638/assets/4942cec8983dd2bddddd0ba2f8f9332b1b82f3999a44adb138b905457d39c633.png)\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{1}{8}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{8}.$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $cos⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{8}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{1}{8})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{1}{64}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{63}{64}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{63}}{8} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{3\\sqrt{7}}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the negative answer since $cos⁡\\theta $ is negative in the second quadrant. So, $cos⁡\\theta =-\\frac{3\\sqrt{7}}{8}.$\n\nFinally, knowing that $sec⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{cos⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\nsec⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{(-\\frac{3\\sqrt{7}}{8})}=-\\frac{8}{3\\sqrt{7}}=-\\frac{8\\sqrt{7}}{21}.\n$$"}]}
